\textbf{Problem Description}

In a deep-sea scientific expedition lasting three days per cycle, a deep-sea observation station must operate continuously for a total of $270$ hours (i.e., $3 \times 90$ hours).

The observation station is equipped with $7$ high-precision environmental sensors, divided into two groups:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Group A (Acoustic Group)}: sensors $A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4$;
    \item \textbf{Group B (Optical Group)}: sensors $A_5, A_6, A_7$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Operating Rules}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Exclusivity}: Throughout the entire $270$-hour mission period, at any given moment, \emph{exactly one} sensor is in operation.
    \item \textbf{Period Constraints}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For each sensor in Group A, the total operating time (measured in hours) must be a positive multiple of $7$.
        \item For each sensor in Group B, the total operating time (measured in hours) must be a positive multiple of $13$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Assuming that the number of on/off switches for each sensor is unrestricted, and considering only the \emph{total cumulative operating time} of each sensor over the entire mission, determine how many distinct allocation schemes are possible.

\textbf{Problem: Maximum Capacity of Non-Dominating Coupled Vectors}

Let $\mathcal{P} = \{p_1, p_2, p_3, \dots\} = \{2, 3, 5, \dots\}$ be the ordered sequence of prime constants. We define a \textit{configuration space} $\mathcal{V}$ consisting of all infinite vectors $\mathbf{v} = (v_1, v_2, \dots)$ with non-negative integer components ($v_i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$), such that only finitely many components are non-zero.

We define a \textit{Cost Function} $C: \mathcal{V} \to \mathbb{N}$ as:
\[ C(\mathbf{v}) = \prod_{i=1}^{\infty} p_i^{v_i} \]

For a fixed capacity threshold $n \ge 2$, let $\Omega_n = \{ \mathbf{v} \in \mathcal{V} \mid C(\mathbf{v}) \le n \}$ be the set of feasible configurations.

We define two relationships between any distinct vectors $\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b} \in \Omega_n$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Vector Dominance ($\preceq$):} We say $\mathbf{a} \preceq \mathbf{b}$ if $a_i \le b_i$ for all indices $i$.
    \item \textbf{Orthogonality ($\perp$):} We say $\mathbf{a} \perp \mathbf{b}$ if their supports are disjoint, i.e., $\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} a_i b_i = 0$.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Objective:}
Determine the maximum cardinality of a subset $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \Omega_n$ subject to the following strict constraints for all distinct pairs $\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in \mathcal{S}$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pareto Efficiency:} Neither vector dominates the other ($\mathbf{u} \npreceq \mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{v} \npreceq \mathbf{u}$).
    \item \textbf{Coupling Requirement:} The vectors must be non-orthogonal ($\mathbf{u} \not\perp \mathbf{v}$).
\end{itemize}

Formally, find:
\[ \max |\mathcal{S}| \]

